  "content": {
    "id": "p2-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Suppose $m$ and $n$ are integers. Prove that $m-n$ and $m+n$ are either both even or both odd.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Both are even when $m$ and $n$ have the same parity; both are odd when their parities differ.\n\nWrite each integer as $2a$ or $2a+1$ and compare the sum and difference.",
      "worked": "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. There are four cases.\n\nIf $m=2a$ and $n=2b$:\n$$\\begin{align*}m-n&=2(a-b)\\\\m+n&=2(a+b)\\end{align*}$$\nBoth are even.\n\nIf $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}m-n&=2(a-b)-1\\\\m+n&=2(a+b)+1\\end{align*}$$\nBoth are odd.\n\nIf $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b$:\n$$\\begin{align*}m-n&=2(a-b)+1\\\\m+n&=2(a+b)+1\\end{align*}$$\nBoth are odd.\n\nIf $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b+1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}m-n&=2(a-b)\\\\m+n&=2(a+b)+2\\end{align*}$$\nBoth are even. Hence the sum and difference always have the same parity.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 140
  },
